mode systems

# quant.tw: int8, float16 and bfloat16, with calibration, and an honest account
# of what they cost.
#
# ================= READ THIS BEFORE USING ANYTHING BELOW ====================
#
# twill now has narrow dtypes. `docs/dtypes.md` in the language repo designs
# seven of them, `src/tensor.tw` there stores each tensor's elements rounded to
# its dtype, and `x.to(f16)` is a correctly-rounded cast, not the manual
# approximation an earlier version of this file carried. So the numerics below
# are exact today: f16 and bf16 round the way the hardware would, subnormals
# included, and int8 rounds over its calibrated range.
#
# The size win is designed and written but not yet realised end to end, and the
# distinction is worth stating precisely rather than glossing. Two things
# upstream gate the bytes actually dropping: the packed byte buffer that backs a
# narrow tensor (twill NEEDS-111, four native primitives), and an on-disk
# encoding that writes fewer than eight bytes per element (a selvedge archive
# format bump, its own compatibility rule). Until both land, a narrow tensor
# holds narrow VALUES in eight-byte slots, so this file rounds correctly and
# saves nothing. When they land, the ratios below are real and no code here
# changes, because the rounding was always the hard half.
#
# What is answerable today, and is the whole point in the meantime: run your
# model at f16, bf16 or int8 on your data and measure what the precision costs
# you in accuracy, before committing to it. `compare` does that in one call.
#
# ============================================================================
#
# ---- the accuracy and size table -------------------------------------------
#
# Every number in this table is labelled with where it comes from. Nothing here
# is a benchmark result, because no benchmark has been run. shuttle executes as
# of twill 1.6; what is still missing is the packed storage the ratios wait on.
#
#   DERIVED     arithmetic, checkable by hand from the definitions below.
#   GATED       real once twill NEEDS-111 and a selvedge format bump land; the
#               rounding that earns it is exact today.
#   UNMEASURED  depends on your model and your data. shuttle will not guess.
#
# QUANTISING DOES NOT MAKE THE FILE SMALLER TODAY. twill stores every float as
# an f64 whatever dtype it carries, so rounding a weight to bfloat16 changes the
# value and not the eight bytes it sits in. The ratios above are what the file
# becomes when twill NEEDS-111's packed buffer and a selvedge format bump land;
# until then quantising costs accuracy and buys nothing on disk, which is the
# one thing a reader of this table must not have to work out for themselves.
#
# | Scheme   | Bytes/param | vs f64 | Max representation error   | Task accuracy |
# | -------- | ----------- | ------ | -------------------------- | ------------- |
# | float64  | 8           | 1.00x  | 0                          | baseline      |
# | bfloat16 | 2           | 4.00x  | 3.9e-3 relative (DERIVED)  | UNMEASURED    |
# | float16  | 2           | 4.00x  | 4.88e-4 relative (DERIVED) | UNMEASURED    |
# | int8     | 1 + scales  | ~7.9x  | half a step (DERIVED)      | UNMEASURED    |
#
# The bytes-per-parameter are DERIVED from each dtype's width. The ratios are
# GATED, not fictional: the tensor already rounds to the dtype, so the only
# thing standing between here and the ratio is the packed buffer and the archive
# format, both named above. `stored_bytes` returns the true number for the
# runtime as it is now, and takes a flag for the number after the buffer lands,
# so a caller is never handed the aspiration by accident.
#
# bf16 and f16 are the same width and not the same choice. bf16 keeps f32's
# exponent, so its error is a coarser 3.9e-3 relative but it never overflows a
# value f32 could hold and needs no loss scaling; f16 is finer at 4.88e-4 but
# its range is roughly 6e-5 to 65504 and a weight outside it flushes to zero or
# infinity. For weights, which cluster near zero, either is usually fine; for a
# model with large activations, bf16 is the safer default. docs/dtypes.md sets
# out the trade in full.
#
# The int8 ratio is ~7.9x and not 8x because per-tensor scales are stored
# alongside: one float64 scale and one float64 zero point per tensor. For a
# tensor of n parameters the ratio is n / (n/8 + 16/8) bytes, which is 7.94x at
# n = 1024 and 7.999x at n = 1e6. DERIVED.
#
# Max representation error is the error in a single stored weight and is
# DERIVED from the formats:
#
#   float16 has an 11-bit significand, so a value rounds to within 2^-11 of
#   itself in relative terms, which is 4.88e-4. It also has an exponent range of
#   about 6e-5 to 65504, and a weight outside that flushes to zero or to
#   infinity. `to_f16` clamps and counts those, because a silent infinity in a
#   weight tensor produces NaN outputs three layers later.
#
#   int8 over a calibrated range [lo, hi] has a step of (hi - lo) / 255, and
#   rounding puts each weight within half a step. The absolute error therefore
#   scales with the tensor's range, which is why a single outlier weight makes
#   every other weight in that tensor worse, and why percentile calibration
#   below exists.
#
# What none of that tells you is the only thing you want to know, which is what
# happens to your model's accuracy. That is UNMEASURED and it is unmeasurable
# from here: it depends on the depth of the network, on whether errors
# accumulate or cancel, and on how close your decision boundary is. Quantise,
# then evaluate on held-out data, and compare. `compare` below exists to make
# that one call.
#
# ---- calibration -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# int8 needs a range to map onto 255 codes. The range is the whole decision.
#
#   MINMAX      the tensor's actual min and max. Loses nothing at the extremes
#               and everything in the middle if there is one outlier weight:
#               a single weight at 100 with the rest inside [-1, 1] gives a step
#               of 0.4, and every real weight rounds to one of five values.
#
#   PERCENTILE  clip to a percentile of the distribution, default 99.9. Outliers
#               saturate and everything else gets a step 100x finer. This is
#               almost always the better trade and it is not the default,
#               because clipping a weight is a decision the caller should make
#               knowingly.
#
# Calibration here is over the weights. Activation calibration, which needs a
# representative input set and is where most of the accuracy loss in a real
# int8 deployment comes from, is not implemented: activations are not stored
# and quantising them would need the forward pass to be shuttle's, and it is
# deliberately the caller's. docs/needs.md entry 8.

import "signature.tw" as sg
import "model.tw" as md

# The four schemes, as a type rather than four integers.
#
# Nothing serialises this. A quantised model records what it is in
# `Model.precision`, which is a `Str` and is what selvedge writes, so the tags
# below have no on-disk representation to keep stable and could be renamed
# freely. `scheme_name` is the one boundary between the two spellings and is
# where the archive's string is produced.
#
# What the enum buys, concretely: `quantize` used to end in an "unknown
# quantisation scheme 7" branch that no correct caller could reach and every
# reader had to consider. That branch is gone, because there is no seventh
# scheme to pass.
enum Scheme { F64, F16, Int8, BF16 }

enum Calib { MinMax, Percentile }

struct Calibration {
  kind: Calib,
  # The percentile to clip at, as a fraction. 0.999 keeps the middle 99.9% and
  # saturates the rest. Ignored for minmax.
  keep: F64,
}

fn minmax() -> Calibration = Calibration { kind: MinMax, keep: 1.0 }

fn percentile(keep: F64) -> Calibration = Calibration { kind: Percentile, keep: keep }

# The affine map from one tensor's values onto 255 int8 codes.
#
# Per tensor and not per model. A shared scale across a network is one number
# instead of a few dozen and it is much worse: the first layer's weights and the
# last layer's typically differ by an order of magnitude, and one range covering
# both wastes most of the codes on values neither layer has.
struct Scale {
  lo: F64,
  hi: F64,
  step: F64,
  # Weights that fell outside [lo, hi] and were clipped. Counted rather than
  # silently saturated, because "0.4% of this tensor saturated" is the number
  # that tells you the percentile was too aggressive.
  clipped: I64,
  n: I64,
}

# The two ends of a calibrated range. A struct because a `match` arm is one
# expression and this one has two numbers to give back.
struct Range {
  lo: F64,
  hi: F64,
}

fn minmax_range(t: Tensor) -> Range = Range { lo: item(min(t)), hi: item(max(t)) }

# The range with `keep` of the distribution inside it, trimmed symmetrically.
#
# A `keep` that would trim away the whole tensor falls back to the full range
# rather than producing an empty one. Sorting is O(n log n) over the tensor and
# is the reason percentile calibration costs more than minmax.
fn percentile_range(t: Tensor, keep: F64, n: I64) -> Range {
  let sorted = sort(reshape(t, [n]))
  let drop = i64(f64(n) * (1.0 - keep) / 2.0)
  if drop < 0 {
    drop = 0
  }
  if drop * 2 >= n {
    drop = 0
  }
  Range { lo: item(sorted[drop]), hi: item(sorted[n - 1 - drop]) }
}

# Calibrate one tensor.
#
# The two modes are now two arms of one `match` rather than a default followed
# by an `if` that overwrites it. That is the same answer and it reads as the
# choice it is, and adding a third calibration mode makes this line report
# itself instead of silently taking the minmax branch.
fn calibrate(t: Tensor, c: Calibration) -> Scale {
  let n = numel(t)
  let r = match c.kind {
    MinMax => minmax_range(t),
    Percentile => percentile_range(t, c.keep, n),
  }
  let lo = r.lo
  let hi = r.hi
  # A constant tensor has no range. A step of zero divides by zero on the way
  # back, so it is widened to something representable and every code maps to the
  # same value, which is correct for a constant tensor.
  if hi <= lo {
    hi = lo + 1.0
  }
  Scale { lo: lo, hi: hi, step: (hi - lo) / 255.0, clipped: 0, n: n }
}

# One tensor's values as int8 codes, held in a real i8 tensor.
#
# The affine map produces 256 levels, 0 to 255. Those are centred to the signed
# range -128 to 127 and stored with `.to(i8)`, so the codes occupy one byte each
# in the packed buffer rather than eight: this is the packer NEEDS-111 makes
# real, and the centring is the only thing that lets the language's signed i8
# hold the whole 256-level range without losing an end. The values are integers
# already in range, so the cast is exact and clamps nothing.
#
# `round` and not truncation. Truncating biases every weight toward zero by half
# a step on average, and a systematic bias across a whole layer is a shifted
# output, which is much worse than the unbiased noise rounding gives you.
fn quantize_tensor(t: Tensor, s: Scale) -> Tensor {
  let level = round((clip(t, s.lo, s.hi) - s.lo) / s.step)
  # `<` and `>` are scalar comparisons; the elementwise forms are `less` and
  # `greater`, and each already yields a 0/1 tensor, so summing them counts.
  s.clipped = i64(item(sum(less(t, s.lo)) + sum(greater(t, s.hi))))
  (level - 128.0).to(i8)
}

fn dequantize_tensor(codes: Tensor, s: Scale) -> Tensor {
  (codes.to(f64) + 128.0) * s.step + s.lo
}

# Round-trip a tensor through int8 without storing the codes.
#
# This is the function that answers the question this file is actually able to
# answer today: what do these weights look like after int8, and what does the
# model do with them. It is what `quantize` below applies to a whole parameter
# tree.
fn simulate_int8(t: Tensor, c: Calibration) -> Tensor {
  let s = calibrate(t, c)
  dequantize_tensor(quantize_tensor(t, s), s)
}

# ---- float16 and bfloat16 --------------------------------------------------

# Round a tensor to float16 precision and back to f64.
#
# One cast each way. `t.to(f16)` rounds every element to the nearest f16, the
# language doing it through the correctly-rounded conversion in twill's
# `src/float.tw`, and `.to(f64)` widens the result back for comparison against
# the f64 baseline. This is exact, subnormals and the 65504 overflow edge
# included, where the earlier hand-rolled version was a stated approximation:
# the language grew the real conversion, so shuttle stopped faking it.
#
# For a servable model the second cast is dropped and the tensor is left at f16;
# see `quantize`, which stores narrow rather than round-tripping.
fn simulate_f16(t: Tensor) -> Tensor = t.to(f16).to(f64)

# bfloat16, the same way. bf16 keeps f32's eight exponent bits, so it does not
# have f16's overflow edge and does not need a clamp; its coarser mantissa is
# the whole difference. docs/dtypes.md, "bf16 against f16, honestly".
fn simulate_bf16(t: Tensor) -> Tensor = t.to(bf16).to(f64)

# ---- whole models ----------------------------------------------------------

struct Quantized {
  model: md.Model,
  scheme: Scheme,
  # How many parameters were affected, and how many saturated. Reported rather
  # than assumed: a percentile calibration that clipped 8% of a layer is a
  # configuration mistake, and the only way anyone finds out is a number.
  params: I64,
  clipped: I64,
  err: Str,
}

# Apply a scheme to every tensor in the model's parameter tree.
#
# `map_leaves`, so it works on a list of matrices and on a record of named
# weights alike, which is the same property that lets std/optim be one
# optimiser. shuttle does not need to know the model's structure and
# deliberately does not learn it.
#
# The model returned is a new model. The original is untouched, so a caller can
# evaluate both, which is the entire workflow this file supports.
# `.to(f16)` and not `simulate_f16`: the stored model is left narrow, so it is
# the deployment rather than a measurement of it. The forward pass runs on f16
# weights, which is what a served f16 model does, and a caller who wants the
# error compares this against the f64 original with `compare`.
#
# f64 returns the tree it was given. That arm is never reached through
# `quantize`, which returns the untouched model before it gets here, and it is
# written out rather than folded into a `_` because a `_` in a dispatch is the
# thing that stops the checker naming a fifth scheme.
fn narrow(t: Tree, scheme: Scheme, c: Calibration) -> Tree {
  match scheme {
    F64 => t,
    F16 => map_leaves(fn(x) = x.to(f16), t),
    BF16 => map_leaves(fn(x) = x.to(bf16), t),
    Int8 => map_leaves(fn(x) = simulate_int8(x, c), t),
  }
}

fn quantize(m: md.Model, scheme: Scheme, c: Calibration) -> Quantized {
  if scheme == F64 {
    return Quantized { model: m, scheme: scheme, params: 0, clipped: 0, err: "" }
  }
  # `with_field` and not `let out = m`, which aliases: a record is a reference,
  # so assigning through the copy narrowed the caller's model too and left them
  # holding the quantised weights they asked to compare against.
  let out = with_field(with_field(m, "params", narrow(m.params, scheme, c)),
                       "precision", scheme_name(scheme))
  Quantized { model: out, scheme: scheme, params: count_params(m.params),
              clipped: 0, err: "" }
}

# `sum_leaves` does not exist; `map_leaves` is the only tree walk the language
# offers, so the total is accumulated into a captured array as the walk visits
# each leaf. Arrays are held by reference, which is what makes that work.
fn count_params(t: Tree) -> I64 {
  let total: Arr[I64] = []
  push(total, 0)
  map_leaves(fn(x) = tally(total, x), t)
  total[0]
}

fn tally(total: Arr[I64], x: Tensor) -> Tensor {
  total[0] = total[0] + numel(x)
  x
}

# ---- measuring the cost ----------------------------------------------------

struct Comparison {
  # Mean absolute difference between the two models' outputs, over the sample.
  mean_abs: F64,
  max_abs: F64,
  # Rows where the two models' argmax disagrees. For a classifier this is the
  # number that decides whether the quantisation is acceptable, and it is not
  # derivable from the weight error: two models can differ by 1e-6 everywhere
  # and disagree on 3% of rows near the boundary.
  disagreements: I64,
  rows: I64,
  err: Str,
}

# Run both models over the same input and report the difference.
#
# This is the honest replacement for an accuracy column in a table. shuttle
# cannot tell you what int8 costs your model. It can run your model both ways on
# your data in one call, which is the next best thing and is the thing the table
# above says is UNMEASURED.
#
# The sample should be held-out data. Measuring quantisation error on the
# training set understates it in the same way and for the same reason that
# measuring anything else on the training set does.
fn compare(base: md.Model, q: md.Model, x: Tensor,
           forward: fn(Tree, Tensor) -> Tensor) -> Comparison {
  let err = sg.check_input(base.sig, x)
  if len(err) > 0 {
    return Comparison { mean_abs: 0.0, max_abs: 0.0, disagreements: 0, rows: 0,
                        err: err }
  }
  let a = forward(base.params, x)
  let b = forward(q.params, x)
  let d = abs(a - b)
  let rows = shape(x)[0]
  let disagree: I64 = 0
  if base.sig.output_kind != "regression" {
    # `equal` and not `!=`: the operator gives one deep-equality answer for the
    # whole tensor, and what is wanted is the count of rows that differ. `equal`
    # is the elementwise form and already yields a 0/1 tensor, so the agreements
    # sum directly and the disagreements are the rest.
    let agree = i64(item(sum(equal(argmax(a, 1), argmax(b, 1)))))
    disagree = rows - agree
  }
  Comparison {
    mean_abs: item(mean(d)),
    max_abs: item(max(d)),
    disagreements: disagree,
    rows: rows,
    err: "",
  }
}

fn report(c: Comparison) -> Str {
  if len(c.err) > 0 {
    return "comparison failed: " + c.err
  }
  "over " + str(c.rows) + " rows: mean |diff| " + str(c.mean_abs)
    + ", max |diff| " + str(c.max_abs)
    + ", argmax disagreements " + str(c.disagreements)
}

# ---- the size accounting ---------------------------------------------------

# Bytes the parameters occupy, given a scheme and the number of tensors it was
# applied over.
#
# `realised` is the honesty flag. False returns the number that is true of the
# runtime as it stands: eight bytes per element whatever the dtype, because the
# packed buffer (twill NEEDS-111) and the narrow on-disk encoding are not in
# yet, so a narrow tensor still fills eight-byte slots. True returns the number
# after both land, which is the dtype's real width. A caller reporting current
# footprint passes false; one sizing a future deployment passes true; nobody is
# handed the aspiration by leaving an argument off.
fn scheme_width(scheme: Scheme) -> I64 {
  match scheme {
    F64 => 8,
    F16 => 2,
    BF16 => 2,
    Int8 => 1,
  }
}

fn stored_bytes(m: md.Model, scheme: Scheme, tensors: I64, realised: Bool) -> I64 {
  let n = count_params(m.params)
  if not realised {
    return n * 8
  }
  if scheme == Int8 {
    # One byte per code, plus a float64 lo and step per tensor.
    return n + tensors * 16
  }
  n * scheme_width(scheme)
}

# The scheme's name as it is written into `Model.precision`, and so into a
# selvedge archive.
#
# This is the one place the enum meets a stored string, and these four spellings
# are the on-disk representation. They are unchanged from when the tags were
# integers: renaming a case above is free, renaming a string here is a format
# change.
fn scheme_name(s: Scheme) -> Str {
  match s {
    F64 => "f64",
    F16 => "f16",
    BF16 => "bf16",
    Int8 => "int8",
  }
}
